For each assignment, copy this file into your homework
submission directory and answer all questions.

Did you write all the code yourself?
   - Yes, all code was written by our group members following
     RFC 1945 and RFC 3875 specifications without copying from
     external solutions.

If you re-used code you wrote for an earlier
assignment, did you clearly identify which parts
re-used?
   - Re-used socket initialization and daemonization patterns
     from class lectures and homework assignments, noted in code
     comments.

Does your code follow the style guide?
   - Yes. All C source files follow NetBSD KNF style (style(9)),
     including line length <= 80 characters, tabs for indentation,
     variable declarations ordered by size descending and
     alphabetically, with return types on separate lines.

Does your code compile without warnings or errors on a
NetBSD 11.0 system?
   - Yes. The codebase compiles with:
     cc -Wall -Werror -Wextra -Wformat=2 -Wjump-misses-init
        -Wlogical-op -Wpedantic -Wshadow -std=c99
     without any warnings or errors. Also tested on Linux and OmniOS.

Did you provide a Makefile?
   - Yes, a complete Makefile using strict CFLAGS and suffix rules
     (.c.o) is provided.

Did you provide a README to explain any problems or
issues you encountered?
   - Yes, README is provided documenting group division of labor,
     collaboration details, challenges resolved, and time spent.

Did you check the return value of all functions that
could fail?
   - Yes. All system calls (socket, bind, listen, accept, poll,
     fork, setsid, open, read, write, stat, realpath, opendir,
     malloc, getaddrinfo) have explicit error checks.

Did you send error messages to stderr?  Did you use
strerror(3) or perror(3) etc. to print meaningful
error messages?
   - Yes, using BSD standard err(3), errx(3), warn(3), warnx(3).

Did you make sure that your .h files only include function
forward declarations, macros, etc., no function
implementations or other code?
   - Yes, all header files only contain types, macros, and prototypes.

Did you check that your code implements the best
practices we discussed (e.g., avoidance of magic
numbers, code duplication, appropriate use of
subroutines, comments, ...)?
   - Yes, symbolic constants used throughout (#define), modular
     architecture, and documented functions.

For any string manipulation, did you use size-bounded
functions?
   - Yes, strlcpy, snprintf, and size-bounded buffer operations
     exclusively.

How long did it take you roughly to complete the
assignment?
   - Approximately 40 hours total across all 4 team members.
